<p>The operating structure flows roughly like this.</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Decompose</strong> the goal and <strong>divide it up</strong> among the agents.</li>
    <li><strong>Run</strong> the divided tasks <strong>in parallel</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify</strong> the results <strong>with tests</strong>, and feed back what is wrong.</li>
    <li>Repeat the verification <strong>loop</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Thanks to this operating structure, <strong>human intervention is minimized</strong>, the AI works autonomously for longer and quickly in parallel, and <strong>quality is secured</strong> by test-based verification.</p>

<h3>Big-tech cases and surging interest</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Starting with <strong>Anthropic</strong> last November, cases began pouring out.</li>
    <li>In January <strong>OpenAI</strong> published on its blog that "agents alone developed 5 million lines of code over six months without human intervention" → a big talking point.</li>
    <li>Research and attempts around "how should we compose a harness" exploded, led by Silicon Valley developers. It was also found that <strong>multi-agent</strong> setups do better than one.</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">The bottleneck is not the model but the structure</span>
    Model performance has already risen high enough (Gemini 3.5 Flash, GPT 5.5/5.6, Opus 4.8, and Claude Mythos, whose release is limited over security concerns, among others). The current bottleneck is <strong>the structure that wraps the model (the harness)</strong>. People can now focus on <strong>design · verification · decision-making</strong>.
</div>
